          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Many of the important theorems of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+theory"} measure theory
            fail to hold in full generality. (See below under Theorems for which theorems we're talking about.) Often these theorems are stated for
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-finite+measures"} finite measures
            %em localizable
            measures, and this fact
            %em characterizes
            the localizable measures.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p The following definition is in elementary terms; but we will see that there are many other characterizations.
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+measure"} positive measure
            on an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abstract+set"} abstract set
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsets"} subsets
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-algebra"} algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+set"} measurable
            by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            maps these sets to the space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,\infty]
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lower+real+numbers"} lower real numbers
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monotone+function"} monotone
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countably+additive+measure"} countably additive
            way.)
          %p
            Given two measurable subsets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %strong essentially contains
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            if the set
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi F
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ x\colon X \;|\; x \in F \;\Rightarrow\; x \in E \}
          %p
            is
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+set"} full
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ∖
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \setminus E
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/null+set"} null
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preorder"} preorder
            on the measurable sets.)
          %p
            Then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            is
            %strong localizable
            if the following conditions both apply:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Semifiniteness: Every measurable set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                with positive measure essentially contains a measurable set with finite positive measure. (We may strengthen ‘essentially contains’ to ‘contains’ in this clause.)
            %li
              %p
                Essential suprema: Given any collection
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                of measurable sets, there is a measurable set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                such that:
              %ul
                %li
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi E
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                  essentially contains each element of
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝒞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                %li
                  Given any measurable set
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                  that essentially contains every element of
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝒞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                  essentially contains
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi E
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              %p
                (This set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                is essentially unique, in that it essentially contains and is essentially contained in any other set with the same property; we call
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/the"} the
                %strong essential union
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ess
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ess \bigcup \mathcal{C}
                of
                = succeed ".)" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
          %p
            We generalize to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measures"} measures
            taking place in some space other than
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,\infty]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            is
            %strong localizable
            if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|\mu|}
            is, as long as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            has an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/absolute+value"} absolute value
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %span.newWikiWord
                total variation
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/total+variation"} ?
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|\mu|}
            that takes values in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,\infty]
          %p
            Of course, a set equipped with a localizable measure is a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong localizable measure space
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            Every
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-finite+measure"} finite
            measure is localizable. (Since this includes so many examples, the theorems below are often stated for
            = succeed "-finite" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
          %p
            Any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counting+measure"} counting measure
            is localizable (but
            = succeed "-finite" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countable+set"} countable set
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
            The following
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
            condition is fundamental: Given any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cover"} cover
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒰
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (a localizable measure space) by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+sets"} measurable sets
            and a
            = succeed "-indexed" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒰
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/family"} family
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+function"} partial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+functions"} measurable functions
            (with
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} dom
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi A
                    %mo =
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dom f_A = A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi A
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_A = f_B
            almost everywhere on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∩
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \cap B
            (meaning that there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subset"} full subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi A
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_A = f_B
            on
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi B
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \cap B \cap E
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/almost+equality"} almost equality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ess
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ess \bigcup f
            such that always
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ess
                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                  %mi f
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ess \bigcup f = f_A
            almost everywhere on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Fremlin"} Fremlin
          %p
            Slightly more generally, start with
            %em any
            family of partial measurable functions on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and treat it as a cover of (any representative of) the essential union of its domains.
          %p
            … other results such as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Radon%E2%80%93Nikodym+theorem"} Radon–Nikodym theorem
            …
          %p
            These ideas are elaborated at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+space"} measurable space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+toposes"} Boolean toposes
            and
            = succeed "s" do
              %span.newWikiWord
                Boolean valued model
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Boolean+valued+model"} ?
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forcing"} forcing
          %h2#other_localizable_structures Other localizable structures
          %p
            Whether a semifinite measure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            is localizable depends only on which measurable sets are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+set"} full
            (or
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/null+set"} null
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+space"} measurable space
            with a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delta-filter"} filter
            of full sets (or a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-ideal"} ideal
            of null sets), without equipping it with the measure of any other set. A
            %strong localizable measurable space
            is a measurable space so equipped, such that every collection of measurable sets has an essential union. (It is a theorem, I believe, that every localizable measurable space is capable of supporting a semifinite, hence localizable, measure with the same full/null sets.)
          %p
            Knowing the full/null sets is also sufficient to define
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/almost+equality"} almost equality
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+functions"} measurable functions
            between measurable spaces; this gives us a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Loc
                  %mi Meas
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Loc Meas
            of localizable measurable spaces. This category is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+category"} dual
            to the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/von+Neumann+algebras"} von Neumann algebras
            %em noncommutative
            localizable measurable space (in the sense of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} noncommutative geometry
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℳ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}
            is the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-algebra"} algebra
            of measurable sets and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℱ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}
            is the
            = succeed "-filter" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{N}
            is the
            = succeed "-ideal" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+ring"} quotient
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boolean+algebra"} boolean algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℳ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi ℱ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}/\mathcal{F}
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℳ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi 𝒩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}/\mathcal{N}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/improper+subset"} entire space
            (or identifying each null set with the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+subset"} empty set
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+boolean+algebra"} complete
          %p
            This boolean algebra is all the structure needed to specify a measure on the original space (at least one that is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/absolutely+continuous+measure"} absolutely continuous
            in that it has at least the requisite full/null sets). We can now abstract away the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/underlying+set"} underlying set
            and simply look at the complete boolean algebra. However, it is
            %em not
            true that every complete boolean algebra is capable of arising in this way from a measurable space. Those which do so arise may be called
            = succeed "," do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+locales"} measurable locales
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frame"} frame
            and measurable functions (up to almost equality) between the measurable spaces correspond to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+maps"} continuous maps
            between the frames, thought of as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locales"} locales
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Loc
                  %mi Meas
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Loc Meas
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalent
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Meas
                    %mi Loc
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Meas Loc
          %p
            Without requiring localizability, the boolean algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℳ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi ℱ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}/\mathcal{F}
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℳ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi 𝒩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}/\mathcal{N}
            = succeed ";" do
              %span.newWikiWord
                measurable algebra
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/measurable+algebra"} ?
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                measure algebra
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/measure+algebra"} ?
            = succeed "," do
              %strong localizable measurable algebra
            %strong localizable measure algebras
            (with the category of these equivalent to the category localizable measure spaces, so long as morphisms in the latter are again only defined up to almost equality).
          %h2#in_weak_foundations In weak foundations
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            is indispensable for the development above, as stated. (The reason is that one constantly makes choices among essentially equivalent measurable sets, or among almost equal measurable functions.) However, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of localizable measurable spaces (and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+functions"} measurable functions
            up to
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/almost+equality"} almost equality
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalent to
            the category of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+locales"} measurable locales
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
          %h2#references References
          %p
            This giant treatise on all of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+theory"} measure theory
            is free (in both senses) online:
          %ul
            %li
              D.H. Fremlin,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Measure Theory
              %a{:href => "https://www1.essex.ac.uk/maths/people/fremlin/mt.htm"} web
        .revisedby
          %p
